# Linux System Information and Resource Monitoring Tool

The Linux System Information and Resource Monitoring Tool is a Linux-based system programming project developed using the C programming language. The main purpose of this project is to monitor the Linux system and its running processes and provide useful information to the administrator through a simple command-line interface.

The system collects important operating system information such as CPU details, memory usage, disk usage, system uptime, and currently running processes. Process information is obtained directly from the Linux `/proc` filesystem, which provides information about processes and system resources.

The tool monitors processes using their Process ID (PID), process name, process state, CPU usage, and memory usage. The administrator can define resource limits for CPU and memory. When a process crosses the specified limit, the system generates a warning so that the administrator can identify processes that are consuming excessive resources.

The project also provides process control using Linux signals. The administrator can stop, continue, or terminate a selected process using signals such as `SIGSTOP`, `SIGCONT`, and `SIGTERM`. This allows the administrator to take action when a process becomes problematic or consumes excessive resources.

The monitoring system also tracks process activity. By comparing process information during different monitoring cycles, it can identify newly created processes and processes that have terminated. Important events such as process creation, termination, resource-limit warnings, and process-control actions are stored in a log file.

The project provides an administrator-controlled monitoring mechanism. The administrator can start monitoring, configure resource thresholds, view running processes, check system information, control processes, view logs, and stop monitoring whenever required.

This project demonstrates important Operating Systems and Systems Programming concepts including Linux process management, `/proc` filesystem, system calls, Linux signals, file handling, CPU and memory monitoring, process states, and resource management. It provides practical experience in developing a system-level application that works directly with Linux operating system facilities.
